(7) All other duties and powers listed in section 2-167(f).
(Code 1977, § 11½-3(b); Ord. No. 05-11, §§ 1, 2, 5-3-2005)

246:
(a) Administration of personnel by city manager. The city manager shall be responsible for a personnel system conforming with the policies and principles established by this division. The city manager shall specifically be responsible for:
(1) Administration of the personnel system;
(2) Selection of a personnel director responsible for the technical direction and operation of the personnel system;
(3) Appointment, removal, suspension and discipline of all employees of the city subject to the policies set forth in this division, the City Charter and state and federal law. This authority may be delegated, at the discretion of the city manager, to the personnel director or other department heads;
(4) The establishment of the number of city employees in the various city departments and offices and determination of duties and compensation of employees in accordance with the policies set forth in this division and subject to budget limitations and approval of the city commission; and
(5) Performance of other duties in personnel administration as may be prescribed by law, this division and policies established hereunder.
The city manager or his authorized agent shall be responsible for certification that the persons employed in the classified service have been appointed and employed in accordance with the provisions of this division and the policies hereunder. No city disbursing or auditing officer shall make or approve or take any part in making or approving any payment for personal service to any person holding a position in the Waycross city government unless said payroll voucher or account of such pay bears the certification of the city manager or his authorized agent.
(b) Administration by personnel director. The city manager shall appoint a personnel director, whose appointment shall be confirmed by the city commission. The personnel director shall:
(1) Administer, under the direction of the city manager, the personnel system as set forth in this division, and the personnel policies;
(2) Recommend to the city manager personnel rules and regulations. Recommend changes to the personnel policies and to this division from time to time for the effective administration of the personnel system;
(3) Maintain the administration of the classification and pay plan;
(4) Develop, coordinate and administer recruiting and training programs for the city and its employees in order to have the best suited employee for a particular job;
(5) Foster and develop programs for the improvement of employee effectiveness including training, safety, health and counseling;
(6) Administer benefits;
(7) Oversee and administer the maintenance of comprehensive personnel records of all the employees of the city;
(8) Direct and administer the affirmative action program of the city;
(9) Make an annual report to the city manager and city commission regarding the work of the department.
(c) Administration of personnel policies, generally.
(1) The personnel policies shall have the force and effect of law. The city manager shall present to the city commission personnel policies, rules and regulations for review and approval by the commission. The policies shall be effective following approval by the commission and after posting the notice and effective date of the proposed personnel policies, additions or changes thereto, for four weeks on the bulletin board or other posting place of every employee work site of the city; and issuance of a memorandum to all employees giving notice of a change in the policies. This memorandum shall be distributed one time only with the employees' paycheck approximately four weeks prior to the effective date of the policy change. During the four-week period, employees may voice any comments regarding the changes in the personnel policies.
(2) The policies will cover specific procedures and policies governing the following phases of the personnel system:
a. Administration of a position classification plan.
b. Administration of a pay plan.
c. Announcement of employee vacancies, acceptance of applications and selection methods.
d. Establishment of promotional policies.
e. Evaluation of the work of employees, including those serving probationary periods.
f. Separation from service of employees by resignation, layoff, suspension and/or dismissal.
g. Establishment of work schedules, attendance and leave regulations.
h. Development of employee training and safety programs.
i. Uniform system of handling all grievances and appeals.
j. Rules governing the outside employment of city employees.
k. Maintenance and use of necessary personnel forms and records.
l. Such other matters as may be necessary to carry out the intent and purpose of this division.
(Code 1977, § 2-180; Ord. No. 79-36, § 1, 12-17-1979)

